/-! # The kernel of Weil restriction and henselian boundary inertia

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0BTD, lemma-normal-pione-quotient-inertia,
and Tag 0BSW, lemma-identify-inertia; blueprint rem-dl-a1-conventions,
second paragraph (blueprint: lem-dl-a1-weil-kernel-inertia).
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory AlgebraicGeometry
open AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.FiniteEtale

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.FiniteEtale

/-- The kernel of restriction is the smallest closed normal subgroup containing the images
of the henselian local inertia kernels at all boundary points.
Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0BTD and 0BSW; rem-dl-a1-conventions, second paragraph
(blueprint: lem-dl-a1-weil-kernel-inertia).

The Weil groups below are the exact fibre products underlying `weilGroup`, as in
`weilGroup_dense_open_surjective`. The pair formula `hρ` characterizes the canonical
restriction and includes its degree compatibility. For each local datum `L x`, the set
in the normal closure is its residue kernel's image under the decomposition map, with
degree zero. No kernel-generation hypothesis occurs in `HenselianBoundaryLocalData`.

Proof, following the node's six steps:
1. Smooth curves are normal by lem-sc-smooth-normal, and finite type over a field makes
them Noetherian by lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian. The normal-component
statement rem-pc-regular-normal and geometric connectedness make C and its dense open
integral. Choose a common separable closure of their function field and an algebraic
closure containing it as geometric generic point.
2. Apply lem-dl-a1-normal-scheme-galois-inertia-presentation (Tag 0BTD) to both schemes.
The two surjections from the absolute Galois group have kernels normally generated by
strict-henselian inertia at their respective points. Functoriality on every finite
etale generic fibre gives b = f a, by lem-galois-pi1-functoriality.
3. Lem-dl-a1-henselian-global-inertia-comparison (Tag 0BSW) identifies each strict-henselian
inertia subgroup with the residue kernel in L x. Its image is exactly the decomposition
image specified by decomposition_spec. Changing its path conjugates that image.
4. Surjectivity of a gives ker f = a(ker b), a closed normal subgroup by compactness.
Generators at points in the open vanish. A closed normal subgroup containing the
boundary images pulls back to one containing all generators of ker b, so ker f is
exactly the closed normal closure of the boundary images in the arithmetic group.
5. By lem-wf-dense-open-pi1-surjective the Weil restriction has the same arithmetic
component and degree coordinate. Its kernel is ker f in degree zero. Boundary inertia
has degree zero by lem-real-boundary-decomposition-landing. Its closed normal closure H
in the Weil group lies in this kernel. The degree-zero subgroup is compact profinite,
so H is compact and its image in the arithmetic group is closed.
6. For any arithmetic g and boundary x, lem-real-boundary-local-constants identifies the
constant-field image of the local decomposition group with Gal(k/k(x)). By
lem-sfb-finite-field-galois-frobenius choose an integer n matching g on k(x), then a local
decomposition element d with arithmetic image r(g) Phi^(-n). Now w = g d^(-1) is a Weil
element. Since local inertia is a normal kernel in its decomposition group,
g I_x g^(-1) = w I_x w^(-1) lies in H. Thus H contains every arithmetic conjugate and,
being closed there, contains ker f by step 4. This proves equality. For empty boundary,
step 4 already gives the trivial kernel and both normal closures are trivial. -/
theorem weilRestriction_ker_eq_henselianBoundaryInertia
    {k₀ : Type u} [Field k₀] [Finite k₀]
    {C U : Scheme.{u}} (p : C ⟶ Spec (.of k₀))
    [Smooth p] [GeometricallyConnected p] [IsSeparated p]
    [LocallyOfFiniteType p] [QuasiCompact p] (hC : C.IsPureDimension 1)
    (j : U ⟶ C) [IsOpenImmersion j] (hj : DenseRange j.base)
    {Ωb : Type u} [Field Ωb] [IsSepClosed Ωb] (b : Spec (.of Ωb) ⟶ U)
    (ρ :
      (constantCoverAction (j ≫ p) b (Aut (fiber b))).weilGroup
        (geometricFrobenius (j ≫ p) b) →*
      (constantCoverAction p (b ≫ j) (Aut (fiber (b ≫ j)))).weilGroup
        (geometricFrobenius p (b ≫ j)))
    (hρ : ∀ w, (ρ w).val = (fiberAutBaseChange j b w.val.1, w.val.2))
    (L : (x : ↥((Set.range j.base)ᶜ)) → HenselianBoundaryLocalData j b x.val) :
    ρ.ker = (Subgroup.normalClosure (⋃ x, {w |
      w.val.2 = 1 ∧ ∃ σ : (L x).Ω ≃ₐ[(L x).K] (L x).Ω,
        (L x).residueAction σ = 1 ∧ (L x).decomposition σ = w.val.1})).topologicalClosure := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.FiniteEtale
